<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Nutrition – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Healthy Food for a Balanced Diet</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret spoken advice on common problems. Interactive: Engage in role-play exercises.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret spoken advice about nutrition and summarize key points about eating balanced meals and local Panamanian foods (e.g., "Summarize advice on eating balanced meals, such as including fresh tropical fruits like mangos or plantains."). Can engage in role-play exercises on healthy eating by offering suggestions (e.g., "You should try adding an apple to your lunch. It's a great source of vitamins and fiber!"). Can engage in discussions by asking questions about food choices (e.g., "Why is it important to eat vegetables every day? What do they do for our bodies?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary related to healthy eating and nutrition.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or images of various foods (e.g., fresh tropical fruits like mangos, plantains, rice, beans, chicken, vegetables, sodas, chips). Students identify each food. The teacher asks, "Which foods are healthy? Which are unhealthy?" and elicits initial responses to activate prior knowledge related to "nutrition" and "healthy eating."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 core vocabulary words on the board (e.g., <b>nutrition</b>, <b>healthy</b>, <b>balanced diet</b>, <b>fruits</b>, <b>vegetables</b>, <b>vitamins</b>, <b>benefits</b>, <b>advise</b>, <b>recommend</b>, <b>eat</b>). The teacher pronounces each word clearly, modeling stress in multisyllabic words like "nutrition" and "vitamins." For "vitamins," the teacher might briefly mention the /ɪ/ sound, contrasting it with /iː/ if a minimal pair naturally arises (e.g., "bit" vs "beat"). The teacher uses simple sentences to explain meaning (e.g., "Nutrition is how food affects our health." "Eating fruits and vegetables has many benefits.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a 'balanced diet'? (Expected: A mix of different healthy foods.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'beneficial,' is it good or bad for you? (Expected: Good.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are some types of 'fruits' we eat in Panama? (Expected: Mango, banana, papaya, etc.)"</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher encourages students to repeat the words for pronunciation practice, focusing on word stress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "Nutrition," focusing on interpreting spoken advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short (1.5-2 minute) audio recording of two people discussing common nutrition problems (e.g., lack of energy, unhealthy snacks) and offering advice. The recording is conversational, a semi-formal dialogue between a nutritionist and a teenager or two friends talking about "healthy food for a balanced diet." It incorporates target vocabulary and grammar (modal "should" for advice, first conditional for facts, adverbial clauses for justification).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets main ideas and specific details in short spoken messages about everyday topics related to health and nutrition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening (1st listen):</b> Students listen to the audio once and answer a general question: "What is the main topic of the conversation? What are the speakers discussing?" (Expected: Healthy eating, nutrition problems, giving advice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (2nd listen):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 simple True/False statements related to the audio's main points. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"The nutritionist advises eating more processed foods. (False)"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"She says you should eat fruits because they give you vitamins. (True)"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"If you eat a balanced breakfast, you will stay focused longer during class. (True)"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (3rd listen):</b> The teacher provides a short list of problems (e.g., "low energy," "craving sweets") and a list of advice (e.g., "eat more grains," "choose fresh fruit"). Students listen again and match each problem to the advice given. The teacher pauses if necessary and replays specific sections.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on interpreting specific details from spoken input and controlled practice of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpreting spoken advice; using modal "should" for giving advice, first conditional for facts, and adverbial clauses for justification; target vocabulary (nutrition, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, vitamins, fiber, protein, carbohydrates, advise, recommend).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Listening (Cloze Activity):</b> The teacher provides a short transcript excerpt from the audio with gaps for key vocabulary words (e.g., "You [should] eat more [fruits] and [vegetables] for a [balanced diet] because they give you [vitamins]."). Students listen to the relevant section(s) of the audio again and fill in the blanks. They check their answers in pairs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Listening (Advice Selection):</b> The teacher presents short scenarios (written or spoken by the teacher, e.g., "Maria feels tired all the time.") and two pieces of advice, only one of which was mentioned in the audio or aligns with healthy eating principles (e.g., a) "She should drink more water." b) "She should eat more sweets."). Students listen to the scenario and choose the best piece of advice based on the audio's content or general nutrition facts. They then complete the sentence "She should _______ because _______." to include an adverbial clause for justification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Dictation & Check):</b> In pairs, one student dictates 2-3 sentences of advice they remember or noted from the audio (e.g., "You should consume more natural foods, like organic fruits."). The partner listens carefully and writes down the sentences. Then, they compare their written sentences with the original audio transcript or the board to check for accuracy, particularly focusing on the correct use of "should," target vocabulary, and pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "organic" or "carbohydrate."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language actively in a realistic scenario, focusing on listening and offering advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs. Students will take turns being "the person with a nutrition problem" and "the health advisor."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides a list of 3-4 common, simple nutrition-related problems (e.g., "I feel sleepy after lunch," "I don't eat enough vegetables," "I prefer processed snacks," "I rarely drink water").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A chooses a problem from the list (or invents one) and explains it to Student B. (e.g., "I feel sleepy after lunch because I eat a lot of fried foods like 'patacones'.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens carefully to the problem and then offers at least two pieces of advice using "You should..." and explains the benefits using "If you..., you will..." or "because it...". (e.g., "You should try adding an apple or a mango to your lunch. It's a great source of vitamins and fiber! If you eat lighter, you will feel more energetic." or "Good nutrition is important because it improves your digestion.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles and repeat the process.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing listening comprehension, the use of target grammar (should, first conditional, adverbial clauses), vocabulary (nutrition, benefits, balanced diet, fruits, vegetables, vitamins, fiber), and pronunciation, especially word stress in "metabolism" or "carbohydrate" if used spontaneously. The teacher reminds students to offer advice on local Panamanian foods where appropriate (e.g., "You should try adding some fresh tropical fruits like mangos or plantains to your breakfast.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of listening comprehension and ability to offer advice, followed by student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during role-play, focusing on listening for understanding and appropriate advice. Quick peer assessment and self-assessment exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will provide a brief oral summary of the advice their partner gave them during the role-play to another pair or the teacher. This demonstrates their listening comprehension and ability to synthesize information. (e.g., "My partner advised me that I should eat more grains because they give me energy.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Student accurately summarizes the advice received.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 3-4 new vocabulary words (e.g., "balanced diet," "vitamins," "benefits," "advise").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Grammar:</b> Correctly uses "should" for advice, and if possible, a first conditional or adverbial clause (e.g., "If you eat X, you will Y," or "because it...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Intelligibility:</b> Pronunciation of target words is clear and understandable, particularly word stress.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active participation and listening during pair work.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned that I should..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned about nutrition is __________, and it means __________."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunity for students and teacher to reflect on the lesson's effectiveness and plan for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening for advice did I find most challenging today? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use the advice from today's lesson to improve my own eating habits?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspects of the listening activities worked well? Were students able to interpret the main ideas and specific details effectively?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future listening tasks, especially regarding the complexity of spoken input or the level of vocabulary?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How was the integration of grammar (should, first conditional, adverbial clauses) and pronunciation (word stress) within the listening focus?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter a written text on healthy eating that uses much of the same vocabulary introduced today, allowing them to deepen their understanding of the context and reinforcing the core lexical items through reading comprehension tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>